## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a beginner-friendly Azure data pipeline that handles schema drift, produces a dimensional model, and captures lineage. It also probes how to isolate raw data, apply transformations, and enforce simple data quality gates with observable provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Schema drift handling in Parquet
- Dimensional modeling (DimUsers, FactEvents)
- Drift capture via JSON blob
- Data quality checks (NOT NULL, timestamp parse)
- Data lineage with Purview integration

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: drift handling in a dataflow step
function transform(record) {
  const known = { user_id, event_time, event_type };
  const drift = _.omit(record, Object.keys(known));
  return { ...record, drift_json: JSON.stringify(drift) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test drift handling with evolving schemas?
- How would you extend this to handle very high throughput volumes?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates the ability to design a comprehensive lakehouse architecture that seamlessly integrates streaming and batch data ingestion while maintaining data quality, governance, and disaster recovery capabilities within the Azure ecosystem.

## Key Concepts

- Delta Live Tables for automated ETL with incremental processing
- Schema drift management through auto-evolution capabilities
- Structured streaming integration from Azure Event Hubs
- Batch data ingestion from SFTP sources
- Data quality enforcement using Delta Live Tables Expectations
- PII protection through custom UDFs and governance controls
- End-to-end lineage tracking with Azure Purview integration
- Cross-region disaster recovery using ADLS Gen2 geo-replication
- Backfill strategies with dedicated pipeline runs

## Code Example

```javascript
// PII masking implementation
function maskPII(record) {
  const r = {...record};
  if (r.email) r.email = r.email.replace(/(.{2}).*(@.*)/, '$1***$2');
  if (r.phone) r.phone = r.phone.replace(/(\d{3})\d{4}(\d{4})/, '$1****$2');
  return r;
}
```

